[SPEAKER_00]: Just, no, I'll just go out and walk.

25
00:02:42,124 --> 00:02:43,645
[SPEAKER_00]: It's kind of... in a way, it's that simple.

26
00:02:44,555 --> 00:02:48,098
[SPEAKER_00]: But it would go against the entire grain of your mind there and then.

27
00:02:50,159 --> 00:02:55,183
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you just... there's... you're trying to just, yeah, stay with comfort.

28
00:02:55,824 --> 00:02:57,565
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't want to... Yeah, yeah.

29
00:02:58,146 --> 00:03:01,328
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's not even... and again, like, see, it can go that far.

30
00:03:02,429 --> 00:03:08,914
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, if you're reaching, like, the levels of, like, chronic depression and so on, you're not even staying with the comfort.

31
00:03:09,415 --> 00:03:09,555
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

32
00:03:09,635 --> 00:03:10,836
[SPEAKER_00]: It's uncomfortable already.

33
00:03:10,976 --> 00:03:11,797
[SPEAKER_00]: It's uncomfortable.

34
00:03:12,632 --> 00:03:24,376
[SPEAKER_00]: But the thought of kind of moving, or at least exerting some, going against some degree of inertia, feels even worse.

35
00:03:24,396 --> 00:03:33,278
[SPEAKER_00]: So, because what you are currently in is less uncomfortable than the projected idea of going against this inertia, that's why I'll just stay with it.

36
00:03:33,658 --> 00:03:34,659
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's uncomfortable.

37
00:03:35,139 --> 00:03:38,880
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not... it's far from being agreeable and pleasant at that point.

38
00:03:39,580 --> 00:03:42,081
[SPEAKER_01]: So if, you know, one is laying around

39
